Gap samples use the first bounded nonempty public deliveries per stream, exclude first delivery, and use a monotonic clock. Gaps include prior hashing, bookkeeping and configured consumer delay. Sample counts, caps, histograms and diagnostic trace units are in the raw sidecars. Quantiles are descriptive samples, not an exhaustive native latency distribution. Three fresh repetitions and CV at most 10% are required for a performance conclusion.

## Load intervals

| client | conc | load seconds min/max | declared duration reached |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
{% for row in aggregate %}
| {{ row.client }} | {{ row.concurrency }} | {{ value(row.load_duration_s_min, 6) }}/{{ value(row.load_duration_s_max, 6) }} | {{ row.duration_window_complete }} |
{% endfor %}

An explicit duration window keeps whole rounds running until the target or hard stream cap. Every completed stream retains its own EOF/framing/length/SHA-256 verdict. The exact whole-window chunk histogram shares a fixed bin budget; detailed delivery records remain globally bounded. A stream-limit stop below the target cannot qualify as a long interval. Warmup stays at fixed rounds. Server body write/drain clocks are optional, intrusive observations and require a separate observer-off control; cumulative completed-body totals and phase deltas are retained raw.

